WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.133
Last clip ended on an empty folder
and one rule: agents move fast,

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:08.133
your rules decide how far. From here
to a working GTM project takes minutes.

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.900
Claude does most of the typing.

00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:12.200
In this clip you'll have
the command line tool,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.267
a project folder Claude understands,
and a check that shows what is live.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.867
Do it by hand and Leo pastes an
agent key into a config file,

00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.100
then commits it. Done properly,
the key never touches a file,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.133
and Orbit starts with the whole playbook.

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:26.300
First, the checklist.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.500
Docker running, Node twenty two or
newer, four gigabytes of free memory,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.733
ten of disk. Claude Code is recommended,
because it runs the setup with you.

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.400
And a second person as approver,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.800
because GTM OS won't let you
approve your own proposals.

00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.400
Two paths from here: connect
to an existing instance,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.433
or run your own on this laptop,
in Docker. You'll see both.

00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:48.100
Step one, a single global npm install.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.867
A project install, or
npx, isn't supported.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.200
Check it with gtm version. Then
make a folder, and run gtm init.

00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:58.200
Step two, the init works
offline, from an empty folder,

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.267
and hands Claude your playbook
before Claude touches anything.

00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:05.333
Here's what lands. Instructions
in CLAUDE. md for Claude, AGENTS.

00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:09.600
md for Codex. A . mcp. json
pointing at the MCP server. A .

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.600
claude folder with
subagents, slash commands,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.000
settings and a session start hook.
Skills for every agent. And docs.

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:21.967
One detail matters: the agent key is
never written to a file, and the . mcp.

00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:25.567
json holds only a reference
to an environment variable.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.500
Now the part the team calls Hermes:

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.233
the setup agents and skills that
gtm init places in your repository.

00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:34.367
A full set of skills, from
onboarding to one per engine,

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:36.733
plus role subagents
like the gtm-architect.

00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:40.967
They land in three places, so Claude,
Codex and other agents all find them.

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:44.167
Checksum verified, with a
bundled copy for offline use.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.667
Operator tip: run gtm init with
refresh when new skills ship.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:52.333
Your edits stay, and gtm doctor warns
you when your skills have gone stale.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.267
Now the gotcha.

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:56.933
Open Claude Code in this folder, and
restart it if it was already open.

00:01:56.933 --> 00:02:00.900
It reads skills only at startup,
so an old session sees nothing new.

00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:03.667
Step three: /setup-gtmos.
Claude runs a runbook.

00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:08.700
It checks Docker, Node, disk and memory,
and looks for a running instance.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.967
Then it asks one grouped
question: organization, owner,

00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:16.467
a different approver, version, ports,
keys. In the console: nothing yet.

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:20.000
Or ask your agent: "Set
up GTM OS for my company".

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:23.400
Then the work starts: the stack
installs in an instance folder,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.967
and your organization is created
with a separate approver.

00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:29.667
Claude mints an agent key
and connects this folder.

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:32.633
It stops only for what a person
must do, like signing in,

00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:35.167
pasting a key or adding DNS records.

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:38.233
Then /build-gtm-system hands
over to the gtm-architect.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.133
Prefer to type it?

00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:43.800
One command does the same: gtm setup,
a folder, your organization name,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.033
owner email and approver email.

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:48.733
Run it twice and the second
run does only what's missing.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.200
Owner and approver passwords,
plus the master key, land in the .

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.767
env in the instance
folder. Keep it private.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.700
That was the Docker path. To connect
to an existing instance instead,

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.767
skip the install and pass its
address and an agent key to gtm init.

00:03:02.833 --> 00:03:07.833
Self hosted, the console, API
and MCP bind to 127. 0. 0. 1.

00:03:07.833 --> 00:03:10.967
By default, only this
machine reaches them.

00:03:10.967 --> 00:03:15.733
To expose them, set GTMOS_BIND_ADDRESS,
or use a reverse proxy.

00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:19.500
Both gtm up and gtm
upgrade warn you first.

00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:21.367
Last step. Run gtm doctor.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:25.567
One line per model provider,
backend, connector and sender,

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:29.800
each reading live, unverified,
failed, fixture or not configured,

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.100
with its exact fix.

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:34.433
A fixture is a sample response
standing in for a real service,

00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:36.300
and it never counts toward your first win.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.400
Wire gtm doctor with
strict into your scripts,

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.333
and it exits with one when
anything needed isn't live.

00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:44.933
Expect the model key
to be missing for now.

00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:47.533
In the console: Integrations, Connections.

00:03:47.533 --> 00:03:50.733
Or ask your agent: "Run gtm doctor
and tell me what is not live".

00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:53.900
Say it out loud: an agent
installing software with my keys?

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.133
It can't approve its own work, because
the approver is a different identity.

00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.033
Nor does it write the key to a file.

00:04:00.033 --> 00:04:03.633
And it stops for every human
step, with every port kept local.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.833
Boiled down: Install globally,
run gtm init, restart Claude,

00:04:08.833 --> 00:04:12.567
run /setup-gtmos. Then gtm doctor.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.367
You have a working project,
an agent with the playbook,

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.700
and a check you can trust.
Keep that doctor output.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.633
After this comes your first
organization and your agent.

00:04:20.633 --> 00:04:22.300
You clear the missing model key,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.533
and the agent gets an
organization of its own.
